This Python function has a bug — it's supposed to flatten a nested dict into dot-separated keys:
```python
def flatten(d, prefix=''):
    items = {}
    for k, v in d.items():
        key = f'{prefix}.{k}' if prefix else k
        if isinstance(v, dict):
            items.update(flatten(v, key))
        else:
            items[key] = v
    return items
```
The bug: it infinite-loops on self-referencing dicts (e.g. d['x'] = d). Fix it so it detects cycles and replaces them with the string '<cycle>' instead of recursing infinitely. Also handle lists by indexing them (e.g. key.0, key.1). Output only the fixed function.

response.text:
```python
def flatten(d, prefix='', _seen=None):
    if _seen is None:
        _seen = set()
    if id(d) in _seen:
        return {'': '<cycle>'} if not prefix else {'': '<cycle>'}
    _seen = _seen | {id(d)}
    items = {}
    for k, v in d.items():
        key = f'{prefix}.{k}' if prefix else k
        if isinstance(v, dict):
            items.update({f'{key}.{kk}' if key else kk: vv
                          for kk, vv in flatten(v, key, _seen).items()})
        elif isinstance(v, list):
            for i, item in enumerate(v):
                if isinstance(item, dict):
                    items.update({f'{key}.{i}.{kk}' if key else f'{i}.{kk}': vv
                                  for kk, vv in flatten(item, f'{key}.{i}', _seen).items()})
                else:
                    items[f'{key}.{i}'] = item
        else:
            items[key] = v
    return items
```
